# Question: what is a customer worth over their lifetime (LTV), how many
# months does it take to recover what we spent acquiring them (CAC payback),
# and how likely is the payback to stay within 12 months?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario product/ltv-cac
# Change an assumption, for example a more expensive acquisition channel:
#   ubc --set 'cac = 1.5k to 4k' --scenario product/ltv-cac
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens payback --scenario product/ltv-cac
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros, periods
# in months. SaaS benchmarks: LTV at least 3 times CAC, payback under 12
# months.
#
# Sources: David Skok, "SaaS Metrics 2.0", For Entrepreneurs
# (forentrepreneurs.com), for LTV at least 3 times CAC and payback within 12
# months as rules of thumb; the ranges are typical experience.

# Assumptions
arpa = 80 to 200                  # monthly revenue per account; read it from your billing
margin = 70% to 85%               # gross margin: revenue left after hosting and support; typical for SaaS
churn = 1% to 4%                  # share of customers who cancel each month; read it from your billing
cac = 500 to 2k                   # sales and marketing spend divided by new customers; from your own books

# Calculation
lifetime_value = ltv(arpa, margin, churn)      # margin over the expected lifetime (1 / churn months)
payback = cac_payback(cac, arpa, margin)       # months until a customer's margin pays back the CAC
ratio = lifetime_value / cac                   # LTV:CAC; above 3 is healthy

# Result
lifetime_value
payback
ratio
P(payback <= 12)                  # how likely is the payback to stay within 12 months?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   lifetime_value = ≈ 4850   90% [2100 … 11k]   98% [1400 … 16k]   ▄▇██▇▅▄▃▃▂▂▁▂▁▁▁
#   payback = ≈ 10.3   90% [4.5 … 23.6]   98% [3 … 34]   ▄▇██▇▅▄▃▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁
#   ratio = ≈ 4.81   90% [1.59 … 14.2]   98% [1 … 24]   ▅█▇▅▄▃▃▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   P(payback <= 12) = 0.62
